# ==========================
# GLOBAL WORKSPACE RULES
# ==========================

You are generating code for a large, multi-tenant SaaS system called TAKA.
All instructions in this workspace must follow these rules.

# ==========================
# 1. CORE PRINCIPLES
# ==========================

- Always respect the architecture and requirements described in /docs/spec.md.
- Before coding any module, ALWAYS read the spec and confirm understanding.
- Work in small, atomic steps. DO NOT generate large patches unless explicitly asked.
- Always show a clear diff preview before applying changes.
- If something is unclear, ALWAYS ask clarifying questions.
- Never invent endpoints, fields, or models not documented in the spec unless asked to.
- Never refactor unrelated code unless explicitly instructed.

# ==========================
# 2. TECH STACK RULES
# ==========================

Backend stack:
- Next.js 15 (App Router)
- TypeScript
- Prisma ORM
- tRPC or Server Actions
- PostgreSQL (Supabase-hosted)

Frontend stack:
- React Server Components
- shadcn/ui
- Tailwind CSS
- Mobile-first UI required

Infrastructure:
- Deployment: Vercel
- Database: Supabase PostgreSQL
- All secrets come from environment variables.

# ==========================
# 3. VERCEL RUNTIME RULES
# ==========================

- All API routes must explicitly set Node.js runtime:

  # Example:
  # export const runtime = "nodejs";

- Do NOT use Edge-incompatible Node APIs:
  # Forbidden:
  # fs, fs/promises
  # worker_threads
  # node-gyp bindings
  # process.nextTick
  # bcrypt native
  # sharp native

- Allowed replacements:
  # bcryptjs instead of bcrypt
  # wasm-compatible sharp if image processing is needed

- Backend logic must work in Vercel Serverless (Node.js runtime).

# ==========================
# 4. DATABASE & PRISMA RULES
# ==========================

- Database provider is Supabase PostgreSQL.
- Must use DATABASE_URL from environment variables.
- Do NOT auto-migrate in Vercel builds.
- Use the standard PrismaClient guard:

  # Example:
  # import { PrismaClient } from "@prisma/client";
  # const globalForPrisma = globalThis as unknown as { prisma: PrismaClient };
  # export const prisma =
  #   globalForPrisma.prisma ||
  #   new PrismaClient({ log: ["query", "info", "warn", "error"] });
  # if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production") globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;

- All tenant data must be scoped with tenantId.
- All Prisma models must match /docs/spec.md exactly.

# ==========================
# 5. ROUTING RULES (Next.js App Router)
# ==========================

- Use the route groups:
  # (public)
  # (tenant)
  # (superadmin)

- Never create two routes that resolve to the same URL.
- Tenant routes require strict authorization checks.
- Public routes must NOT import server-only code.

# ==========================
# 6. SECURITY RULES
# ==========================

- Authentication uses secure cookies.
- Server must always validate user/tenant context.
- Never trust client-sent headers without server verification.
- API responses must use consistent error structure.
- Never leak sensitive data to client.

# ==========================
# 7. UI RULES
# ==========================

- Use server components by default.
- Add "use client" only when required.
- Follow shadcn/ui conventions strictly.
- All pages must be fully responsive (mobile-first).

# ==========================
# 8. BUILD COMPATIBILITY RULES
# ==========================

- All code must compile on Vercel without modification.
- When a Vercel build error appears, the agent must:
  - Identify the failing file(s),
  - Generate the smallest required fix,
  - Ensure changes remain aligned with /docs/spec.md,
  - Request approval before applying large structural changes.

# ==========================
# 9. WORKFLOW RULES
# ==========================

- All changes must be incremental and explicitly approved.
- Code must be typed, readable, and consistent.
- No dead code or unused imports.
- The agent must ask for confirmation when:
  - Deleting any file,
  - Renaming any file,
  - Modifying authentication logic,
  - Changing multi-tenant architecture,
  - Adding new database models